WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.280
In this video, you'll go from zero to your first FedEx test label in about fifteen minutes — and everything stays in FedEx's sandbox.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:18.042
First, install and activate the plugin — the full walkthrough is in the Installation tutorial. After activation, a FedEx Shipping menu appears.

00:00:18.492 --> 00:00:26.460
Along the way, you'll need sandbox API keys from your FedEx developer project, and a shipping zone that covers the address you'll test with.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:37.638
In FedEx Shipping, open Settings, enter your Client ID and Client Secret on the Authorization tab, set the Account tab's Environment to Sandbox, and click Save Changes.

00:00:38.088 --> 00:00:47.400
Next, open WooCommerce, then Settings, then Shipping, and add the Fedex Shipping method to your zone. Its settings box is empty — that's normal.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:59.370
Next, open WooCommerce, then Settings, then Shipping, and add the Fedex Shipping method to your zone. Its window has just an on/off switch and a title — nothing FedEx-specific.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:06.444
The plugin is in instant mode by default, so the label is created automatically the moment the order is placed.

00:01:06.894 --> 00:01:14.166
Your test shipment then appears in Shipments and Printings with its tracking number — open or print the label with View Label.

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:23.232
The Track view shows the shipment's status timeline — and about five minutes later, the customer email follows with the tracking number and a label link.

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:31.602
If a rate or a label doesn't appear, start with Troubleshooting. And when you're ready for real shipping, the Going live tutorial takes you there.

